# ============================================================================
# Valgrind suppressions for known false positives.
# ============================================================================
#
# These suppressions do NOT hide bugs in this project's code.
# They silence issues in library / platform code that Helgrind cannot
# correctly model.
#
# Each suppression documents:
#   - what triggers it
#   - why it is not a real bug
#   - a reference for further reading
#
# ============================================================================


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# glibc condition variable wake-chain internal signal (Misc variant)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Since glibc 2.25, pthread_cond_signal uses a "wake chain" optimization:
# the woken waiter may signal the next waiter in the same group while
# re-acquiring the user's mutex, before that mutex is held by any thread.
# Helgrind sees this internal signal as a pthread_cond contract violation.
#
# Our queue holds the mutex for every user-visible signal/broadcast
# (verified by inspection of queue.tpp). The report originates entirely
# inside glibc's __pthread_cond_wait_common.
#
# The trailing '...' matches any stack frames below the glibc boundary.
# This anchors the suppression to glibc's internal behavior, not to any
# specific call site in our code.
#
# Reference:
#   https://sourceware.org/glibc/wiki/CondVar
{
   glibc_condvar_internal_signal_misc
   Helgrind:Misc
   obj:/usr/libexec/valgrind/vgpreload_helgrind-amd64-linux.so
   fun:__pthread_cond_wait_common
   ...
}


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# glibc condition variable wake-chain internal signal (Dubious variant)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Same underlying glibc behavior as above, reported under a stricter
# Helgrind category ('Dubious' = "associated lock is not held by any
# thread"). Helgrind uses this category when it can prove the signal is
# emitted from within the pthread_cond_wait resume path.
#
# Reference:
#   https://sourceware.org/glibc/wiki/CondVar
{
   glibc_condvar_internal_signal_dubious
   Helgrind:Dubious
   obj:/usr/libexec/valgrind/vgpreload_helgrind-amd64-linux.so
   fun:__pthread_cond_wait_common
   ...
}